  {
   "node_id": "inf:coefficients-algebraic",
   "node_type": "Inference",
   "properties": {
    "confirmed_how": "unconfirmed",
    "name": "Step 2: under the supposition, p's coefficients are algebraic",
    "rule": "all",
    "rule_name": "unfolding the definition (with the supposition assumed)",
    "warrant": "The coefficient 1 is algebraic: it is the root of x \u2212 1. The coefficient \u03c0\u00b7e is algebraic by the supposition. The coefficient \u2212(\u03c0+e) is algebraic: by the supposition \u03c0+e is a root of some nonzero rational polynomial f, and then g(x) = f(\u2212x) is a nonzero rational polynomial with g(\u2212(\u03c0+e)) = 0. (The cited field property gives the same at once.)"
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "inf:expand-p",
   "node_type": "Inference",
   "properties": {
    "confirmed_how": "unconfirmed",
    "name": "Step 1: expand (x \u2212 \u03c0)(x \u2212 e)",
    "rule": "all",
    "rule_name": "direct computation (expanding a product by the field laws)",
    "warrant": "By distributivity and commutativity in the complex numbers, (x \u2212 \u03c0)(x \u2212 e) = x\u00b2 \u2212 e\u00b7x \u2212 \u03c0\u00b7x + \u03c0\u00b7e = x\u00b2 \u2212 (\u03c0+e)\u00b7x + \u03c0\u00b7e for every complex x. Putting x = \u03c0 makes the left side 0, so p(\u03c0) = 0; and p is monic of degree 2, so it is not the zero polynomial. Uses nothing about \u03c0 or e except that they are complex numbers."
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "inf:pi-algebraic",
   "node_type": "Inference",
   "properties": {
    "confirmed_how": "unconfirmed",
    "name": "Step 3: \u03c0 is a root of a polynomial with algebraic coefficients, so \u03c0 is algebraic",
    "rule": "all",
    "rule_name": "modus ponens (instance of the cited closure theorem)",
    "warrant": "The cited theorem says: any complex root of a nonzero polynomial whose coefficients are algebraic numbers is algebraic. Lemma 1 gives that p is nonzero and p(\u03c0) = 0; Lemma 2 gives that p's coefficients are algebraic (under the supposition). So, under the supposition, \u03c0 is algebraic."
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "inf:reductio",
   "node_type": "Inference",
   "properties": {
    "confirmed_how": "unconfirmed",
    "discharges": [
     "req:suppose-both-algebraic"
    ],
    "name": "Step 4: reductio, discharging the supposition",
    "rule": "all",
    "rule_name": "reductio ad absurdum (negation introduction), then De Morgan through the definition of transcendental",
    "warrant": "Under the supposition \u03c0 is algebraic (Lemma 3), yet Lindemann's theorem says \u03c0 is not algebraic: a contradiction. So the supposition is false: \u03c0 + e and \u03c0\u00b7e are not both algebraic. By the definition of transcendental (= not algebraic), at least one of them is transcendental, which is the theorem. The contradiction is drawn inside this step; it is not recorded as a statement of its own."
   }
  },
  {
