    "mode": "flexible",
    "name": "pi + e and pi times e (proof trial)",
    "objective": "A proof that at least one of pi + e and pi times e is transcendental, a person can read and check, with every step's form checked by the tool",
    "status": "active"
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "q:contradiction-inside-the-last-step",
   "node_type": "Question",
   "properties": {
    "answer": "One step is fine: keep the contradiction drawn inside the reductio step. The missing way to state a contradiction goes on reflow2's fix list.",
    "answered_at": "2026-10-18",
    "answered_by": "who:acct-23984879ca",
    "answered_via": "who:agent-d82ad83a71",
    "asked_of": "who:acct-23984879ca",
    "context_setter": "Step 4 (the reductio) draws the contradiction 'pi is algebraic and not algebraic' inside the step and concludes the theorem directly, discharging the supposition that both numbers are algebraic.",
    "question": "Is the last step acceptable as one step (contradiction, then 'not both algebraic', then 'at least one transcendental' by the definition)? Or do you want it split into a recorded contradiction statement and a separate reductio step, so each move can be challenged on its own?",
    "rephrase_degraded": false,
    "status": "answered"
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "q:take-cited-results-as-given",
   "node_type": "Question",
   "properties": {
    "answer": "Take both as given: Lindemann (1882) and the algebraic closure of the algebraic numbers (Dedekind 1871; Lang), each with its source as recorded.",
    "answered_at": "2026-10-18",
    "answered_by": "who:acct-23984879ca",
    "answered_via": "who:agent-d82ad83a71",
    "asked_of": "who:acct-23984879ca",
    "context_setter": "The proof that pi + e or pi*e is transcendental rests on two results it cites without proving: Lindemann's 1882 theorem that pi is transcendental, and the standard fact that the algebraic numbers form an algebraically closed field.",
    "question": "Do you take both cited results as given, as recorded? Or should the closure fact (a root of a polynomial with algebraic coefficients is algebraic) be proved inside this design from the tower law, so the proof rests only on Lindemann? And is a textbook (e.g. Lang, Algebra, ch. V) an acceptable source for it, or do you want the original (Dedekind 1871)?",
    "rephrase_degraded": false,
    "status": "answered"
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "req:algebraic-numbers-closed",
   "node_type": "Requirement",
   "properties": {
    "lineage": "original",
    "name": "Cited: the algebraic numbers are a field, and algebraically closed",
    "priority": "medium",
    "provenance": "authored",
    "source": "Standard algebraic number theory. Field property: R. Dedekind, supplements to Dirichlet's 'Vorlesungen uber Zahlentheorie' (2nd ed., 1871). Root-of-algebraic-coefficients: finite-extension (tower law) argument, e.g. S. Lang, 'Algebra', Ch. V (Algebraic extensions). Cited, not proved here.",
    "statement": "The algebraic numbers form a subfield of the complex numbers (sums, differences, products and quotients of algebraic numbers are algebraic), and any complex root of a nonzero polynomial whose coefficients are algebraic numbers is itself algebraic.",
    "statement_kind": "theorem",
    "status": "proposed",
    "taken_as_given": true
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "req:lemma-pi-root-of-p",
   "node_type": "Requirement",
   "properties": {
    "lineage": "original",
    "name": "Lemma 1: \u03c0 is a root of x\u00b2 \u2212 (\u03c0+e)x + \u03c0e",
    "priority": "medium",
    "provenance": "authored",
    "statement": "For every complex x, x\u00b2 \u2212 (\u03c0+e)\u00b7x + \u03c0\u00b7e = (x \u2212 \u03c0)(x \u2212 e). Hence the polynomial p(x) = x\u00b2 \u2212 (\u03c0+e)\u00b7x + \u03c0\u00b7e is nonzero (it is monic, of degree 2) and p(\u03c0) = 0. (This holds unconditionally: it does not use the supposition.)",
    "statement_kind": "lemma",
    "status": "proposed"
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "req:lindemann-pi-transcendental",
   "node_type": "Requirement",
   "properties": {
    "lineage": "original",
    "name": "Cited: Lindemann's theorem, \u03c0 is transcendental",
    "priority": "medium",
    "provenance": "authored",
    "source": "Ferdinand von Lindemann, 'Uber die Zahl \u03c0', Mathematische Annalen 20 (1882), 213-225. Cited, not proved here. (Hermite proved e transcendental in 1873; that is not needed.)",
    "statement": "\u03c0 is transcendental: it is the root of no nonzero polynomial with rational coefficients.",
    "statement_kind": "theorem",
    "status": "proposed",
    "taken_as_given": true
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "req:p-coefficients-algebraic",
   "node_type": "Requirement",
   "properties": {
    "distinct_from": [
     "req:lemma-pi-root-of-p"
    ],
    "lineage": "original",
    "name": "Lemma 2 (under the supposition): p has algebraic coefficients",
    "priority": "medium",
    "provenance": "authored",
    "statement": "Under the supposition, the coefficients of p(x) = x\u00b2 \u2212 (\u03c0+e)\u00b7x + \u03c0\u00b7e, namely 1, \u2212(\u03c0+e) and \u03c0\u00b7e, are all algebraic numbers.",
    "status": "proposed"
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "req:pi-algebraic",
   "node_type": "Requirement",
   "properties": {
    "lineage": "original",
    "name": "Lemma 3 (under the supposition): \u03c0 is algebraic",
    "priority": "medium",
    "provenance": "authored",
    "statement": "Under the supposition, \u03c0 is an algebraic number.",
    "statement_kind": "lemma",
    "status": "proposed"
   }
  },
  {
   "node_id": "req:suppose-both-algebraic",
   "node_type": "Requirement",
   "properties": {
    "lineage": "original",
    "name": "Supposition (for contradiction): \u03c0 + e and \u03c0\u00b7e are both algebraic",
    "priority": "medium",
    "provenance": "authored",
    "source": "The proof's own supposition: the negation of the theorem, by the definition of transcendental.",
